// Libre AI Biscuit authority block template v2.
// Extends v1 with the loop-security kernel K1 agent identity taxonomy
// (docs/specifications/LOOP-SECURITY-KERNEL.md, realized at the orchestrator
// lock). Values are issuer-side parameters. No PII or content is permitted.
//
// Human and browser-session tokens continue to use the v1 authority block.
// Agent-fleet tokens (the self-feeding loop, opened at the wave-3 lock) use
// this v2 block, which binds the agent to exactly one fleet and to an explicit
// capability scope.

user({user});
tenant({tenant});
role({user}, {role});

// K1 — agent identity taxonomy. An agent belongs to exactly one fleet and
// operates within one mission (mission binding is carried by token_mission in
// the agent-runs authorizer). capability_scope is the explicit permission
// vector: which tools and write paths, never CI or gates by default.
agent_fleet({agent}, {fleet});
capability_scope({agent}, {capability});

// An agent token must carry its identity: no fleet, no authority.
check if agent_fleet($agent, $fleet);

// Expiry (unchanged from v1).
check if time($time), $time < {expires_at};

// Enforcement notes (not expressible in the block, enforced by authz-biscuit):
// - Cross-fleet and cross-mission operation is denied by the agent-runs policy
//   authorizer (deny-by-default; allow rules require matching token_mission /
//   resource_mission and the fleet-scoped role).
// - Revocation is per agent_id, not per token: a revoked agent issues no new
//   tokens, and the revocation store fails closed (unavailable => deny),
//   consistent with the G1 Biscuit revocation doctrine.
// - capability_scope is checked at the tool/write-path boundary, never granting
//   CI or gate write by default.
